{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KNLHFc1j3SENz9tKzBehyh","type":"tool_result","content":"115\t## Anti-patterns — do NOT do these\n116\t\n117\t* **Don't stub a body as `Err(Error::Generic)` and move on.**  This\n118\t  silently hides untranslated work behind a generic-error\n119\t  unwrap-panic and looks \"done\" to the gate.  If you genuinely\n120\t  cannot translate a body, leave the original `todo!()` in place\n121\t  so a future pass can pick it up — that is the visible-work\n122\t  contract.  Err-stubs are forbidden.\n123\t* **Don't return `Ok(())` as a no-op for functions that have real\n124\t  side effects in C** (running test scenarios, building scenario\n125\t  state, walking qlog files, etc.).  Same problem — looks \"done\"\n126\t  but isn't.\n127\t* **Don't fabricate return values.**  If the body needs to read a\n128\t  qlog file and report a numeric result, write the qlog reader.\n129\t  Don't return `0` and pretend.\n130\t\n131\tIf a body is genuinely beyond reach in this pass (e.g. it depends\n132\ton a sub-system that's not translated yet), stop translating it\n133\tand stdout-flag it — but leave the `todo!()` intact.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## The \"tests are the gate\" workflow\n138\t\n139\tFor each translated function:\n140\t\n141\t1. **Find the tests that exercise it.**  `grep -lE \"<fn name>\" rs/fq/src/tests/`\n142\t   often points right at them.  The Phase 3 known-answer tests\n143\t   (intformat, varint, siphash, splay, hashtest, code_version)\n144\t   exercise data-structure leaves first.\n145\t2. **Translate the body.**  Use Edit; preserve the existing\n146\t   signature and doc comment.  Don't rename / reshape.\n147\t3. **Run `cargo test <test_name>`.**  If it still panics, the\n148\t   panic backtrace tells you which other `todo!()` is in the\n149\t   way.  Translate that next, then rerun.\n150\t4. **Repeat until the test passes.**  Then move to the next test.\n151\t\n152\tBottom-up order is the natural one.  In the Phase 0 call graph,\n153\tfunction-level \"max height\" gives the depth.  Leaves first means\n154\tdata-structure tests light up first; connection-lifecycle tests\n155\tlast.\n156\t\n157\t---\n158\t\n159\t## When to add new types\n160\t\n161\t* The C type catalog is in [`crate::internal`] (private structs\n162\t  internal to the library) and [`crate::lib`] (public types\n163\t  re-exported at the crate root).  These are stable; don't add\n164\t  fields unless absolutely necessary.\n165\t* If the C body needs a `static` table (e.g. cipher-suite\n166\t  registries, frame-name tables, per-version parameter blocks),\n167\t  put it in the same module as the function that consumes it,\n168\t  as a `pub(crate) const` or `static`.\n169\t* Helpers that are only used by one function: nest with `fn`\n170\t  inside that function or as a private `fn` immediately above.\n171\t  Keep the public surface lean.\n172\t\n173\t---\n174\t\n175\t## Conservative refactors allowed\n176\t\n177\tThe Phase 4 prompt forbids \"rename / reshape\" of existing\n178\titems.  But these refactors are explicitly allowed:\n179\t\n180\t* **Splitting a long fn into helpers** when the C body has a\n181\t  natural segmentation (e.g. parse → validate → record).  Keep\n182\t  the public surface — the helpers are private.\n183\t* **Replacing a manual loop with an iterator chain** when the\n184\t  semantics are exactly preserved.\n185\t* **Replacing a manual `for` with `.copy_from_slice`,\n186\t  `.iter().sum()`, `.windows(N)`, etc.**\n187\t* **Changing local variable types** (e.g. `usize` → `u32` if the\n188\t  range fits) when the body's invariants justify it.\n189\t\n190\tIf a refactor crosses a public-API boundary (changing a\n191\tparameter type, adding a return value, splitting one fn into\n192\ttwo public ones), surface it on stdout and stop — that's a\n193\thuman design call.\n194\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"995c99e7-9103-4cf3-9072-dd220baa16c1","uuid":"4f722599-157c-4e7e-805e-d82700911c54","timestamp":"2026-05-06T03:46:43.324Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/xlate/impl_translation_guide.md","content":"## Anti-patterns — do NOT do these\n\n* **Don't stub a body as `Err(Error::Generic)` and move on.**  This\n  silently hides untranslated work behind a generic-error\n  unwrap-panic and looks \"done\" to the gate.  If you genuinely\n  cannot translate a body, leave the original `todo!()` in place\n  so a future pass can pick it up — that is the visible-work\n  contract.  Err-stubs are forbidden.\n* **Don't return `Ok(())` as a no-op for functions that have real\n  side effects in C** (running test scenarios, building scenario\n  state, walking qlog files, etc.).  Same problem — looks \"done\"\n  but isn't.\n* **Don't fabricate return values.**  If the body needs to read a\n  qlog file and report a numeric result, write the qlog reader.\n  Don't return `0` and pretend.\n\nIf a body is genuinely beyond reach in this pass (e.g. it depends\non a sub-system that's not translated yet), stop translating it\nand stdout-flag it — but leave the `todo!()` intact.\n\n---\n\n## The \"tests are the gate\" workflow\n\nFor each translated function:\n\n1. **Find the tests that exercise it.**  `grep -lE \"<fn name>\" rs/fq/src/tests/`\n   often points right at them.  The Phase 3 known-answer tests\n   (intformat, varint, siphash, splay, hashtest, code_version)\n   exercise data-structure leaves first.\n2. **Translate the body.**  Use Edit; preserve the existing\n   signature and doc comment.  Don't rename / reshape.\n3. **Run `cargo test <test_name>`.**  If it still panics, the\n   panic backtrace tells you which other `todo!()` is in the\n   way.  Translate that next, then rerun.\n4. **Repeat until the test passes.**  Then move to the next test.\n\nBottom-up order is the natural one.  In the Phase 0 call graph,\nfunction-level \"max height\" gives the depth.  Leaves first means\ndata-structure tests light up first; connection-lifecycle tests\nlast.\n\n---\n\n## When to add new types\n\n* The C type catalog is in [`crate::internal`] (private structs\n  internal to the library) and [`crate::lib`] (public types\n  re-exported at the crate root).  These are stable; don't add\n  fields unless absolutely necessary.\n* If the C body needs a `static` table (e.g. cipher-suite\n  registries, frame-name tables, per-version parameter blocks),\n  put it in the same module as the function that consumes it,\n  as a `pub(crate) const` or `static`.\n* Helpers that are only used by one function: nest with `fn`\n  inside that function or as a private `fn` immediately above.\n  Keep the public surface lean.\n\n---\n\n## Conservative refactors allowed\n\nThe Phase 4 prompt forbids \"rename / reshape\" of existing\nitems.  But these refactors are explicitly allowed:\n\n* **Splitting a long fn into helpers** when the C body has a\n  natural segmentation (e.g. parse → validate → record).  Keep\n  the public surface — the helpers are private.\n* **Replacing a manual loop with an iterator chain** when the\n  semantics are exactly preserved.\n* **Replacing a manual `for` with `.copy_from_slice`,\n  `.iter().sum()`, `.windows(N)`, etc.**\n* **Changing local variable types** (e.g. `usize` → `u32` if the\n  range fits) when the body's invariants justify it.\n\nIf a refactor crosses a public-API boundary (changing a\nparameter type, adding a return value, splitting one fn into\ntwo public ones), surface it on stdout and stop — that's a\nhuman design call.\n","numLines":80,"startLine":115,"totalLines":228}}}
